@node AGI LOAD
@subsection AGI LOAD

Save game data to persistent storage (disk, NVRAM, or cartridge).

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
LOAD "@var{filename}"
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

The LOAD command retrieves previously saved game state from storage media. In a real AGI system,
this would restore variables, flags, object positions, and inventory state. On modern targets,
this maps to cartridge or flash storage read operations.

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item Filename: DISPLAY (ASCII-encoded string)
@item Slot index: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-255)
@item Return status: BINARY UNSIGNED (0=success, 1=failure, 2=corrupted)
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
@comment Load game from slot 0
LOAD "save_slot_0"
@end example

@example
@comment Conditional load with error checking
IF (load_result = 0)
  PRINT "Game loaded successfully"
ELSE
  PRINT "Load failed, check save file"
END-IF
@end example

@example
@comment Load and verify game checksum
LOAD "autosave"
VAR game_checksum = 42
IF (game_checksum = 42)
  PRINT "Checksum verified"
ELSE
  PRINT "Save file corrupted"
END-IF
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Storage Limitations}: Original AGI uses 32KB save slots. Plan variable usage accordingly.
@item @strong{Platform Differences}: Some platforms use battery-backed RAM; others use disk or cartridge storage.
@item @strong{Game State Versioning}: If you add new variables, old save files won't contain them.
  Initialize new variables to defaults after loading.
@item @strong{Atomic Operations}: LOAD is atomic; partial loads don't occur. Check the return status to determine success.
@item @strong{Save File Format}: Sierra used proprietary binary format with checksums. Respect file format when implementing loaders.
@end itemize

@node AGI SAVE
@subsection AGI SAVE

Load previously saved game state from storage.

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
SAVE "@var{filename}"
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

The SAVE command persists the current game state (all variables, flags, object positions, inventory)
to persistent storage. This is the complementary operation to LOAD. In real AGI implementations,
SAVE writes to disk; in EIGHTBOL targets, it maps to cartridge flash or NVRAM.

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item Filename: DISPLAY (ASCII string, max 32 chars)
@item Slot index: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-255)
@item Return status: BINARY UNSIGNED (0=success, 1=disk full, 2=write error)
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
@comment Save game to slot 1
SAVE "save_slot_1"
@end example

@example
@comment Autosave with timestamp
IF (game_progress > 50)
  SAVE "autosave_checkpoint"
  PRINT "Game saved at checkpoint"
END-IF
@end example

@example
@comment Save with result checking
VAR save_result = 0
SAVE "quicksave"
IF (save_result = 0)
  PRINT "Save complete"
ELSE
  PRINT "Save failed - storage full?"
END-IF
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Storage Capacity}: Each save slot is typically 32KB. Monitor free space before saving on cartridge targets.
@item @strong{No Compression}: Original AGI saves use raw binary format; no compression is applied.
@item @strong{Save File Location}: On modern platforms, respect the system's save directory conventions.
@item @strong{Simultaneous Access}: Do not attempt SAVE and LOAD on the same file simultaneously.
@item @strong{Checksum Validation}: Include checksum validation in loader to detect corrupted saves.
@end itemize

@node AGI DISPLAY
@subsection AGI DISPLAY

Display text or graphics on screen with optional formatting.

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
DISPLAY "@var{text}" AT @var{row}, @var{column}
@end defsyn

@defsyn
DISPLAY @var{image_resource}
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

DISPLAY outputs text strings or graphic resources to the screen. On classic AGI systems, this renders
directly to the video memory. For EIGHTBOL targets, this maps to the platform's graphics output.

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item Text: DISPLAY (ASCII string)
@item Row: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-25, valid for 320x200 mode)
@item Column: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-40)
@item Image resource ID: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-255)
@item Color: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-15 in 16-color mode, 0-255 in 256-color mode)
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
@comment Display text at screen position (5, 10)
DISPLAY "Welcome to the Game" AT 5, 10
@end example

@example
@comment Display a graphic resource
VAR room_image = 42
DISPLAY room_image
@end example

@example
@comment Conditional display with variable substitution
IF (player_health > 0)
  DISPLAY "Health: " AT 0, 0
ELSE
  DISPLAY "GAME OVER" AT 12, 16
END-IF
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Screen Coordinates}: Row 0 is top; Column 0 is left. AGI uses character coordinates, not pixel coordinates.
@item @strong{Text Clipping}: Text that extends beyond screen right edge is clipped or wrapped depending on platform.
@item @strong{Resource Dependencies}: Graphic resources must be loaded before display. Check resource manager state.
@item @strong{Performance}: Frequent DISPLAY calls can cause screen flicker on real hardware. Buffer updates when possible.
@item @strong{Color Limitations}: 16-color and 256-color palettes vary by AGI version. Design with conservative color use.
@end itemize

@node AGI PRINT
@subsection AGI PRINT

Print formatted text to screen or output device.

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
PRINT "@var{text}"
@end defsyn

@defsyn
PRINT "@var{format-string}" WITH @var{value1}, @var{value2}, ...
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

PRINT outputs text to standard output. Unlike DISPLAY, PRINT does not specify screen position and
writes to the current cursor position. This is useful for debug output and simple messaging.

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item Text: DISPLAY (string literal or variable)
@item Format specifiers: @code{%d} (decimal), @code{%x} (hexadecimal), @code{%s} (string), @code{%b} (binary)
@item Values: BINARY (unsigned), BINARY SIGNED (signed), DISPLAY (strings)
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
@comment Simple print
PRINT "You see a treasure chest"
@end example

@example
@comment Print with variable substitution
VAR player_level = 5
PRINT "Your level: %d" WITH player_level
@end example

@example
@comment Print multiple values
VAR health = 100
VAR mana = 50
PRINT "HP: %d, Mana: %d" WITH health, mana
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Output Buffering}: On real systems, PRINT output is buffered. Flush after critical messages.
@item @strong{Format String Safety}: Ensure format strings have exactly as many specifiers as provided values.
@item @strong{Newline Handling}: PRINT automatically adds newline on most platforms; explicit control varies.
@item @strong{Max Line Length}: Most AGI implementations limit lines to 80 characters.
@item @strong{No Printf Expansion}: Format strings don't support @code{%c} or floating-point formats.
@end itemize

@node AGI INPUT
@subsection AGI INPUT

Read player input from keyboard, joystick, or controller.

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
INPUT @var{input_variable} FROM @var{input_source}
@end defsyn

@defsyn
INPUT @var{input_variable} TIMEOUT @var{seconds}
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

INPUT waits for player input (keyboard or joystick) and stores the result in a variable.
The input can be a direction code, key code, or parsed command. INPUT with TIMEOUT allows
non-blocking input with a fallback after specified seconds.

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item Input variable: BINARY UNSIGNED (key code 0-127, or direction 0-8)
@item Input source: BINARY UNSIGNED (0=keyboard, 1=joystick, 2=mouse)
@item Timeout: DECIMAL (seconds, 0-255)
@item Return status: BINARY UNSIGNED (0=input received, 1=timeout)
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
@comment Wait for any key input
INPUT player_key FROM keyboard
IF (player_key = 27)
  PRINT "ESC pressed"
ELSE
  PRINT "Key code: %d" WITH player_key
END-IF
@end example

@example
@comment Wait for direction input with timeout
INPUT direction TIMEOUT 5
IF (direction = 0)
  PRINT "No input received"
ELSE
  PRINT "Player moved"
END-IF
@end example

@example
@comment Poll joystick input
INPUT joystick_state FROM joystick
IF (joystick_state = 4)
  PRINT "Joystick up"
END-IF
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Blocking Behavior}: INPUT blocks game execution until player provides input (unless TIMEOUT used).
@item @strong{Direction Codes}: 1-8 for compass directions (1=NW, 2=N, 3=NE, etc.), 0=center/no direction.
@item @strong{Key Codes}: Standard ASCII 32-126 for printable characters; special codes 0-31 for control keys.
@item @strong{Joystick Availability}: Not all platforms support joystick. Gracefully degrade to keyboard.
@item @strong{Input Buffer}: Some implementations buffer multiple key presses; others discard while blocked.
@end itemize

@node AGI IF Statement
@subsection AGI IF Statement

Conditional branching based on flag or variable values.

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
IF @var{condition}
  @var{statements}
[ELSE
  @var{statements}]
[END-IF]
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

The IF statement evaluates a condition and executes different code branches based on the result.
Conditions can test flags, compare variables, or combine multiple conditions with AND/OR/NOT.

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item Condition operands: BINARY UNSIGNED (0=false, non-zero=true)
@item Comparison results: BINARY UNSIGNED (0 or 1)
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
@comment Simple flag test
IF (game_started)
  PRINT "Game is running"
END-IF
@end example

@example
@comment Variable comparison with else branch
VAR player_score = 150
IF (player_score > 100)
  PRINT "Congratulations!"
ELSE
  PRINT "Try again"
END-IF
@end example

@example
@comment Complex condition with AND/OR
IF (player_alive AND (inventory_count > 0 OR inventory_count = 0))
  PRINT "Inventory status OK"
END-IF
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Short-Circuit Evaluation}: AND/OR operators may not evaluate all operands; rely on evaluation order.
@item @strong{Nesting}: IF statements can be nested; use proper indentation and END-IF markers for clarity.
@item @strong{Dangling Else}: Ambiguous else-if chains may misbehave; always use explicit END-IF markers.
@item @strong{Operator Precedence}: NOT binds tighter than AND, which binds tighter than OR.
@item @strong{Assignment in Conditions}: Using @code{=} for assignment in condition behaves correctly; result is assigned value.
@end itemize

@node AGI GOTO
@subsection AGI GOTO

Unconditional jump to labeled statement.

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
GOTO @var{label}
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

GOTO transfers control unconditionally to the specified label. Labels are defined by name followed by colon.
GOTO is useful for implementing loops and complex control flow, though it can reduce code clarity.

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item Label: Identifier (symbolic reference)
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
@comment Simple loop using GOTO
init_loop:
  VAR counter = counter + 1
  IF (counter < 10)
    GOTO init_loop
  END-IF
@end example

@example
@comment Jump to error handler
IF (error_condition)
  GOTO error_handler
END-IF
PRINT "Normal execution"
error_handler:
  PRINT "Error occurred"
@end example

@example
@comment Multiple GOTO targets
VAR state = 0
IF (state = 0)
  GOTO state_zero
ELSE
  GOTO state_one
END-IF
state_zero:
  PRINT "State 0"
state_one:
  PRINT "State 1"
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Backward Jumps}: GOTO can jump backward, creating loops. Ensure exit conditions exist.
@item @strong{Forward Jumps}: Jumping past variable declarations may cause uninitialized access.
@item @strong{Label Scope}: Labels are global within a routine; use unique names to avoid conflicts.
@item @strong{Stack Unwinding}: GOTO does not unwind stack frames; use RETURN for proper cleanup.
@item @strong{Code Maintenance}: Excessive GOTO usage (goto-hell) reduces code clarity; use sparingly.
@end itemize

@node AGI GOSUB
@subsection AGI GOSUB

Call a subroutine and save return address on stack.

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
GOSUB @var{subroutine_label}
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

GOSUB transfers control to a subroutine while saving the current program counter on a call stack.
The subroutine must end with RETURN to resume execution at the instruction following GOSUB.
GOSUB enables code reuse and modularity.

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item Subroutine label: Identifier (symbolic reference)
@item Return address: BINARY (stored on stack, platform-dependent width)
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
@comment Simple subroutine call
GOSUB print_welcome
PRINT "Game started"

print_welcome:
  PRINT "Welcome to the Adventure"
  RETURN
@end example

@example
@comment Recursive subroutine call (countdown)
VAR countdown_value = 5
GOSUB countdown
PRINT "Liftoff!"

countdown:
  IF (countdown_value > 0)
    PRINT countdown_value
    DECREMENT countdown_value
    GOSUB countdown
  END-IF
  RETURN
@end example

@example
@comment Nested GOSUB calls
GOSUB initialize_game
GOSUB main_loop

initialize_game:
  GOSUB load_resources
  GOSUB setup_player
  RETURN

load_resources:
  PRINT "Loading resources..."
  RETURN

setup_player:
  PRINT "Setting up player..."
  RETURN

main_loop:
  PRINT "Game loop running"
  RETURN
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Stack Overflow}: Deep recursion can overflow the call stack. Most AGI implementations limit call depth.
@item @strong{Return Correctness}: Every subroutine path must end with RETURN; missing RETURN causes undefined behavior.
@item @strong{Stack Frame Management}: Each GOSUB allocates stack space for return address and locals.
@item @strong{Tail Call Optimization}: Not typically implemented in AGI; recursive calls consume stack linearly.
@item @strong{Parameter Passing}: AGI uses global variables for parameter passing, not stack-based calling conventions.
@end itemize

@node AGI RETURN
@subsection AGI RETURN

Return from subroutine to caller.

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
RETURN
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

RETURN pops the return address from the call stack and transfers control back to the instruction
following the GOSUB that called the current subroutine. Every subroutine must end with RETURN.

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item Return address: BINARY (restored from stack)
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
@comment Simple return
my_routine:
  PRINT "Inside routine"
  RETURN
@end example

@example
@comment Conditional return
check_status:
  IF (player_health <= 0)
    PRINT "Player defeated"
    RETURN
  END-IF
  PRINT "Player alive"
  RETURN
@end example

@example
@comment Multiple return paths
divide_numbers:
  IF (divisor = 0)
    PRINT "Error: division by zero"
    RETURN
  END-IF
  VAR result = dividend / divisor
  PRINT "Result: %d" WITH result
  RETURN
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Missing RETURN}: Subroutines without explicit RETURN may fall through to next routine or crash.
@item @strong{Return Value}: AGI does not support function return values; use global variables instead.
@item @strong{Early Return}: Multiple RETURNs in different branches is acceptable and common.
@item @strong{Stack Cleanup}: RETURN assumes call stack is properly maintained; GOTO bypassing GOSUB breaks it.
@item @strong{Main Program}: The main program flow does not require RETURN; only subroutines do.
@end itemize

@node AGI FLAG Operations
@subsection AGI FLAG Operations

Set, reset, and check boolean flag values.

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
SET @var{flag_name}
@end defsyn

@defsyn
RESET @var{flag_name}
@end defsyn

@defsyn
IF (@var{flag_name})
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

FLAGS are single-bit boolean values stored efficiently in bit arrays. SET initializes a flag to 1 (true),
RESET initializes to 0 (false). Flags are typically used for game state tracking (game_started, player_alive, etc.).

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item Flag value: BINARY UNSIGNED (0=false, 1=true)
@item Flag index: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-255 flags per 256-flag bank)
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
@comment Set and test a flag
SET game_started
IF (game_started)
  PRINT "Game has begun"
END-IF
@end example

@example
@comment Flag reset
SET player_alive
@comment ... gameplay ...
RESET player_alive
PRINT "Game Over"
@end example

@example
@comment Multiple flag operations
SET visited_room_1
SET found_key
SET puzzle_solved
IF (visited_room_1 AND found_key AND puzzle_solved)
  PRINT "Congratulations!"
END-IF
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Efficiency}: FLAGS use 1 bit per flag; extremely efficient for boolean state.
@item @strong{Limited Flags}: Original AGI supports 256 flags; careful allocation is necessary.
@item @strong{Flag Names}: Use descriptive names (use_key_on_door) rather than cryptic ones (f42).
@item @strong{Initialization}: Flags are uninitialized at startup; explicitly SET or RESET all used flags.
@item @strong{Persistence}: Flags are saved with game state; consider initial values when loading.
@end itemize

@node AGI VAR Assignment
@subsection AGI VAR Assignment

Assign values to integer variables.

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
VAR @var{variable_name} = @var{value}
@end defsyn

@defsyn
@var{variable_name} = @var{expression}
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

Variable assignment stores integer values in named storage locations. Variables can store any integer
value within the range of the target platform (typically 0-255 for 8-bit, 0-65535 for 16-bit).

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item Variables: BINARY UNSIGNED (default) or BINARY SIGNED
@item Values: BINARY, DECIMAL, or DISPLAY numeric types (automatically converted)
@item Range: 0-255 (8-bit), 0-65535 (16-bit), 0-2^32-1 (32-bit)
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
@comment Simple assignment
VAR player_score = 0
@end example

@example
@comment Assignment from expression
VAR health = 100
VAR max_health = health * 2
@end example

@example
@comment Assignment with type conversion
VAR hex_value = x'FF'
VAR binary_value = b'11110000'
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Overflow}: Assignment of values exceeding range truncates or wraps silently.
@item @strong{Uninitialized Variables}: Variables are uninitialized at startup; always assign before use.
@item @strong{Variable Scope}: Variables are global within a program; naming conflicts require prefixes.
@item @strong{Memory Usage}: Each variable typically uses 1-4 bytes; conserve for constrained platforms.
@item @strong{Performance}: Variable access is O(1); no performance penalty for large variable pools.
@end itemize

@node AGI VAR Comparison
@subsection AGI VAR Comparison

Compare integer variables and expressions.

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
IF (@var{variable} = @var{value})
@end defsyn

@defsyn
IF (@var{variable} < @var{value})
@end defsyn

@defsyn
IF (@var{variable} > @var{value})
@end defsyn

@defsyn
IF (@var{variable} <= @var{value})
@end defsyn

@defsyn
IF (@var{variable} >= @var{value})
@end defsyn

@defsyn
IF (@var{variable} <> @var{value})
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

Variable comparison operators test the relationship between two values. All comparison operators
return 0 (false) or 1 (true), which can be stored in variables or used in IF conditions.

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item Operands: BINARY UNSIGNED, BINARY SIGNED, or expressions combining both
@item Result: BINARY UNSIGNED (0=false, 1=true)
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
@comment Equality comparison
IF (player_score = 1000)
  PRINT "High score!"
END-IF
@end example

@example
@comment Range checking
IF (player_level > 10 AND player_level < 20)
  PRINT "Mid-level player"
END-IF
@end example

@example
@comment Store comparison result
VAR is_powerful = (player_strength > 50)
IF (is_powerful)
  PRINT "Player is strong"
END-IF
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Sign Coercion}: Comparing BINARY and BINARY SIGNED may produce unexpected results if sign bits differ.
@item @strong{Operator Precedence}: Comparison operators have lower precedence than arithmetic; use parentheses for clarity.
@item @strong{Chained Comparisons}: @code{1 < x < 10} is not supported; use @code{(x > 1) AND (x < 10)} instead.
@item @strong{Floating Point}: No floating-point comparisons; all arithmetic is integer-only.
@item @strong{Not-Equal}: @code{<>} operator tests inequality; equivalent to @code{NOT (a = b)}.
@end itemize

@node AGI Arithmetic Operations
@subsection AGI Arithmetic Operations

Perform integer arithmetic: addition, subtraction, multiplication, division.

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
@var{result} = @var{operand1} + @var{operand2}
@end defsyn

@defsyn
@var{result} = @var{operand1} - @var{operand2}
@end defsyn

@defsyn
@var{result} = @var{operand1} * @var{operand2}
@end defsyn

@defsyn
@var{result} = @var{operand1} / @var{operand2}
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

Arithmetic operations combine two operands using addition, subtraction, multiplication, or division.
Results are integer; division truncates toward zero.

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item Operands: BINARY UNSIGNED or BINARY SIGNED
@item Result: BINARY UNSIGNED (default) or BINARY SIGNED (if either operand is signed)
@item Range: 0-255, 0-65535, or 0-2^32-1 depending on platform
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
@comment Addition
VAR total = 50 + 25
@end example

@example
@comment Subtraction with underflow
VAR delta = 10 - 20
@comment Result is 256 - 10 = 246 in 8-bit unsigned (wraps)
@end example

@example
@comment Multiplication
VAR product = 12 * 8
@end example

@example
@comment Division with truncation
VAR quotient = 25 / 3
@comment Result is 8 (25 div 3)
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Overflow}: Results exceeding max value silently wrap or truncate.
@item @strong{Underflow}: Subtracting larger from smaller unsigned value wraps modulo 2^N.
@item @strong{Division by Zero}: Dividing by zero produces platform-specific behavior (may crash or return 0).
@item @strong{Integer Division}: No remainder operation; @code{25 / 3 = 8}, not 8.333.
@item @strong{Operator Precedence}: @code{*} and @code{/} bind tighter than @code{+} and @code{-}.
@end itemize

@node AGI Logical Operations
@subsection AGI Logical Operations

Combine conditions using AND, OR, and NOT operators.

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
IF (@var{condition1} AND @var{condition2})
@end defsyn

@defsyn
IF (@var{condition1} OR @var{condition2})
@end defsyn

@defsyn
IF (NOT @var{condition})
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

Logical operators combine conditions: AND returns true if both operands are true, OR returns true
if either operand is true, NOT returns the opposite of its operand.

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item Operands: BINARY UNSIGNED (0=false, non-zero=true)
@item Result: BINARY UNSIGNED (0=false, 1=true)
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
@comment AND condition
IF (player_alive AND has_weapon)
  PRINT "Ready to fight"
END-IF
@end example

@example
@comment OR condition
IF (is_night OR is_dark_room)
  PRINT "Visibility reduced"
END-IF
@end example

@example
@comment NOT condition
IF (NOT player_dead)
  PRINT "Game continues"
END-IF
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Short-Circuit Evaluation}: Some implementations short-circuit AND/OR; later operands may not evaluate.
@item @strong{Precedence}: NOT > AND > OR.
@item @strong{Parentheses}: Use explicit parentheses to avoid precedence ambiguity.
@item @strong{De Morgan's Laws}: @code{NOT (A AND B) = (NOT A) OR (NOT B)}.
@item @strong{Boolean Context}: Any non-zero value is considered true; 0 is false.
@end itemize

@node AGI SOUND
@subsection AGI SOUND

Play, stop, or control sound effects and music.

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
SOUND PLAY @var{sound_id}
@end defsyn

@defsyn
SOUND STOP @var{sound_id}
@end defsyn

@defsyn
SOUND VOLUME @var{level}
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

The SOUND command manages audio output. SOUND PLAY starts playback of a sound resource by ID.
SOUND STOP halts playback. SOUND VOLUME controls output level (0=silent, 255=max).

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item Sound ID: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-255)
@item Volume level: BINARY UNSIGNED (0=off, 255=maximum)
@item Duration: DECIMAL (seconds, 0-255)
@item Channel: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-3 typical)
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
@comment Play sound effect
SOUND PLAY 5
@end example

@example
@comment Stop all audio
SOUND STOP 0
@end example

@example
@comment Adjust volume
SOUND VOLUME 200
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Limited Channels}: Typically 4 sound channels; playback may be limited.
@item @strong{Priority}: High-priority sounds may interrupt lower-priority ones.
@item @strong{Resource Management}: Ensure sound resources are loaded before playback.
@item @strong{Synchronization}: Sound playback is asynchronous; games must track completion.
@item @strong{Mixer Limitations}: Audio mixing quality depends on hardware; plan for degradation.
@end itemize

@node AGI OBJECT Commands
@subsection AGI OBJECT Commands

Manage game objects: positioning, visibility, animation state.

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
OBJECT @var{object_id} AT @var{x}, @var{y}
@end defsyn

@defsyn
OBJECT @var{object_id} ANIMATE
@end defsyn

@defsyn
OBJECT @var{object_id} SHOW
@end defsyn

@defsyn
OBJECT @var{object_id} HIDE
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

OBJECT commands control non-player character (NPC) and animated object behavior. Operations include
positioning (AT), animation (ANIMATE), and visibility (SHOW/HIDE).

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item Object ID: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-255)
@item X coordinate: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-319 for 320-pixel width)
@item Y coordinate: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-199 for 200-pixel height)
@item Animation frame: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-15)
@item Visibility flag: BINARY UNSIGNED (0=hidden, 1=visible)
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
@comment Position object at coordinates
OBJECT 2 AT 160, 100
@end example

@example
@comment Animate object
OBJECT 2 ANIMATE
@end example

@example
@comment Show/hide object
OBJECT 2 SHOW
OBJECT 2 HIDE
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Object Limits}: Original AGI supports 16-255 objects; tracking becomes difficult beyond 32.
@item @strong{Coordinate System}: (0,0) is top-left; Y increases downward.
@item @strong{Animation Frames}: Animations are cycled automatically; frame time depends on system timer.
@item @strong{Collision Detection}: AGI does not provide built-in collision; implement manually.
@item @strong{Depth Ordering}: Objects are drawn in ID order; manage layering by ID assignment.
@end itemize

@node SCI Commands Reference
@section SCI Commands Reference

Sierra Script (SCI) provides an object-oriented scripting layer built on the Sierra Creative Interpreter engine.
SCI enables advanced game features including class hierarchies, methods, and property access.

@menu
* SCI Method Definition::
* SCI Method Calls::
* SCI Property Access::
* SCI Conditionals::
* SCI Loops::
* SCI Variables::
* SCI Array Access::
* SCI String Operations::
* SCI Control Flow::
@end menu

@node SCI Method Definition
@subsection SCI Method Definition

Define methods within classes or standalone procedures.

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
(method (@var{self} @var{arg1} @var{arg2} ...)
  @var{body}
)
@end defsyn

@defsyn
(procedure (@var{arg1} @var{arg2} ...)
  @var{body}
)
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

Methods are functions bound to classes; procedures are standalone functions. Methods receive @code{self}
as the first parameter (reference to the object). Both accept arguments and return values.

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item @code{self}: Object reference (typically BINARY pointer)
@item Arguments: BINARY UNSIGNED, BINARY SIGNED, DISPLAY, or object references
@item Return value: Same types as arguments
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
(method (init str)
  (= name str)
  (= health 100)
  self
)
@end example

@example
(procedure (add-numbers x y)
  (+ x y)
)
@end example

@example
(method (take-damage damage)
  (-= health damage)
  (if (< health 0)
    (= health 0)
  )
  health
)
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Implicit Return}: The value of the last expression is implicitly returned.
@item @strong{Parameter Passing}: Arguments are passed by value (or reference for objects).
@item @strong{Local Variables}: Use @code{local} keyword for stack-allocated variables.
@item @strong{Method Resolution}: SCI uses dynamic dispatch; method calls resolve at runtime.
@item @strong{Tail Recursion}: Not optimized; deep recursion can overflow stack.
@end itemize

@node SCI Method Calls
@subsection SCI Method Calls

Call methods on objects or standalone procedures.

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
(send @var{object} @var{method} @var{arg1} @var{arg2} ...)
@end defsyn

@defsyn
(@var{procedure-name} @var{arg1} @var{arg2} ...)
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

Method calls use the @code{send} operator to invoke methods on objects. Procedure calls use direct function call syntax.
Both support arbitrary argument counts and return values.

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item Object reference: BINARY pointer
@item Method name: Symbol (identifier)
@item Arguments: Mixed types (BINARY, DISPLAY, objects)
@item Return value: Varies by method
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
(send player init "Player")
@end example

@example
(send player take-damage 20)
@end example

@example
(add-numbers 5 7)
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Runtime Dispatch}: Method resolution happens at runtime; no compile-time verification.
@item @strong{Method Not Found}: Calling non-existent methods produces runtime errors.
@item @strong{Argument Mismatch}: No type checking; passing wrong argument types causes silent failures.
@item @strong{Chained Sends}: @code{(send (send obj method1) method2)} chains method calls.
@item @strong{Implicit Self}: Within a method, use @code{send self} or shorthand syntax.
@end itemize

@node SCI Property Access
@subsection SCI Property Access

Access and modify object properties.

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
(property (@var{prop1} @var{initial1})
  (@var{prop2} @var{initial2})
)
@end defsyn

@defsyn
(@var{object} get @var{property-name})
@end defsyn

@defsyn
(@var{object} set @var{property-name} @var{value})
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

Properties are data members of objects. They can be declared with initial values and accessed
via @code{get} and @code{set} methods (or direct field access in some SCI variants).

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item Property value: BINARY UNSIGNED, BINARY SIGNED, DISPLAY, or object reference
@item Initial value: Compile-time constant or expression
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
(class Player
  (property
    (name "Player")
    (health 100)
    (mana 50)
  )
)
@end example

@example
(= (player get 'health) 100)
@end example

@example
(= player-health (player get 'health))
(if (> player-health 50)
  (print "Good health")
)
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Initialization}: Properties are initialized at object creation time.
@item @strong{Scope}: Properties are object-scoped; different instances have separate copies.
@item @strong{Access Control}: SCI supports public/private property modifiers.
@item @strong{Lazy Initialization}: Some properties may be initialized on first access.
@item @strong{Memory}: Each property consumes object memory; minimize property counts.
@end itemize

@node SCI Conditionals
@subsection SCI Conditionals

Conditional branching using @code{if}, @code{cond}, and other constructs.

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
(if @var{condition}
  @var{then-branch}
  @var{else-branch}
)
@end defsyn

@defsyn
(cond
  (@var{condition1} @var{action1})
  (@var{condition2} @var{action2})
)
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

SCI conditionals test conditions and execute different code branches. @code{if} is binary (true/false);
@code{cond} supports multiple conditions (like switch/case).

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item Condition: BINARY UNSIGNED (0=false, non-zero=true)
@item Action: Any expression or sequence of expressions
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
(if (> health 0)
  (print "Alive")
  (print "Dead")
)
@end example

@example
(cond
  ((= level 1) (print "Beginner"))
  ((= level 2) (print "Intermediate"))
  ((= level 3) (print "Expert"))
)
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Truthiness}: 0 is false; any other value is true.
@item @strong{Nested Conditions}: Nesting @code{if}/@code{cond} is natural and encouraged.
@item @strong{Missing Else}: @code{if} without else branch returns 0 (false).
@item @strong{Cond Default}: Add final @code{(TRUE action)} for default case.
@item @strong{Side Effects}: Conditions may have side effects; order matters.
@end itemize

@node SCI Loops
@subsection SCI Loops

Iterate using @code{for}, @code{while}, @code{repeat}, and @code{forall}.

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
(for (local i 0) (< i 10) (++ i)
  @var{body}
)
@end defsyn

@defsyn
(while @var{condition}
  @var{body}
)
@end defsyn

@defsyn
(repeat (= i (+ i 1))
  (if (> i 10) (break))
)
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

SCI loops enable repetitive execution of code blocks. @code{for} loops have initialization, condition, and increment.
@code{while} loops repeat while condition is true. @code{repeat} loops iterate until explicit @code{break}.

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item Loop variable: BINARY UNSIGNED or BINARY SIGNED
@item Condition: BINARY UNSIGNED (0=false, non-zero=true)
@item Counter: BINARY (typically 8 or 16-bit)
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
(for (local i 0) (< i 100) (++ i)
  (print i)
)
@end example

@example
(while (> health 0)
  (send player take-damage 1)
)
@end example

@example
(repeat (= enemies-killed (+ enemies-killed 1))
  (if (> enemies-killed 10)
    (break)
  )
)
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Break/Continue}: @code{break} exits loop; @code{continue} skips to next iteration.
@item @strong{Infinite Loops}: Loops without exit conditions hang; ensure termination.
@item @strong{Off-by-One}: Common error: @code{(for (local i 0) (<= i 10) ...))} includes 0-10 inclusive (11 iterations).
@item @strong{Loop Variable Scope}: Loop variables in @code{for} are scoped to the loop.
@item @strong{Performance}: Deep nested loops may cause performance issues; optimize hot paths.
@end itemize

@node SCI Variables
@subsection SCI Variables

Declare and use local and global variables.

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
(local @var{var1} @var{var2} ...)
@end defsyn

@defsyn
(global @var{global-var})
@end defsyn

@defsyn
(= @var{variable} @var{value})
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

@code{local} declares stack-allocated variables with function scope. @code{global} declares module-level variables.
Assignment uses @code{=} operator.

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item Local variables: BINARY UNSIGNED, BINARY SIGNED, or object references
@item Global variables: Same types as local variables
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
(procedure (calculate)
  (local result temp1 temp2)
  (= result (+ temp1 temp2))
  result
)
@end example

@example
(global game-state)
(= game-state 0)
@end example

@example
(local
  (score 0)
  (health 100)
  (alive 1)
)
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Uninitialized Variables}: Local variables are uninitialized; always initialize before use.
@item @strong{Scope}: Local variables are function-scoped; globals are module-scoped.
@item @strong{Shadowing}: Local variables can shadow global variables.
@item @strong{Memory}: Each variable consumes stack space; minimize local variable counts.
@item @strong{Initialization}: Initialize locals in declaration or at function start.
@end itemize

@node SCI Array Access
@subsection SCI Array Access

Create and access array elements.

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
(local array-var)
(= array-var (new Array))
([array-var 0] 10)
@end defsyn

@defsyn
([array-var 0])
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

Arrays are dynamically allocated objects. Use square bracket notation @code{[array index]} to access elements.
Elements can be any SCI value type.

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item Array size: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-65535)
@item Index: BINARY UNSIGNED (must be < size)
@item Element: BINARY UNSIGNED, BINARY SIGNED, DISPLAY, or object
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
(local inventory)
(= inventory (new Array 10))
([inventory 0] "Sword")
([inventory 1] "Shield")
@end example

@example
(for (local i 0) (< i 10) (++ i)
  (print ([inventory i]))
)
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Bounds Checking}: No automatic bounds checking; array access out of bounds corrupts memory.
@item @strong{Type Safety}: Array elements are untyped; mixing types causes type errors at runtime.
@item @strong{Size}: Fixed at creation; resizing requires creating new array and copying data.
@item @strong{Performance}: Array access is O(1); use arrays for collections of any size.
@item @strong{Memory}: Arrays consume heap memory; large arrays in tight loop may cause garbage collection.
@end itemize

@node SCI String Operations
@subsection SCI String Operations

Create, manipulate, and compare strings.

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
(local str)
(= str "Hello World")
(strcat str " suffix")
(strlen str)
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

Strings are sequences of characters stored as DISPLAY values. SCI provides string manipulation procedures:
@code{strcat} concatenates, @code{strlen} returns length, @code{strcmp} compares, @code{strcpy} copies.

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item String: DISPLAY (ASCII or extended ASCII)
@item Length: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-65535)
@item Comparison result: BINARY SIGNED (-1=less, 0=equal, 1=greater)
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
(local name location)
(= name "Player")
(= location "Forest")
(print (strcat name " is in " location))
@end example

@example
(if (strcmp name "Player")
  (print "Welcome back, Player")
)
@end example

@example
(local msg)
(= msg "Hello")
(print (strlen msg))
@comment Prints: 5
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Null Termination}: Strings are null-terminated; avoid raw buffer manipulation.
@item @strong{Memory}: String operations may allocate temporary buffers; minimize string operations in hot loops.
@item @strong{Case Sensitivity}: String comparisons are case-sensitive; use @code{strcasecmp} for case-insensitive.
@item @strong{Buffer Overflow}: @code{strcpy} without bounds checking can overflow buffers.
@item @strong{Encoding}: SCI strings use ASCII/extended ASCII; Unicode not directly supported.
@end itemize

@node SCI Control Flow
@subsection SCI Control Flow

Control execution flow using @code{switch}, @code{cond}, @code{return}, and @code{throw}.

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
(switch @var{expression}
  (case @var{value1} @var{action1})
  (case @var{value2} @var{action2})
)
@end defsyn

@defsyn
(return @var{value})
@end defsyn

@defsyn
(throw @var{exception-type})
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

@code{switch} dispatches to different code branches based on expression value. @code{return} exits function
with specified value. @code{throw} signals exception (error condition).

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item Expression: BINARY UNSIGNED, BINARY SIGNED, or DISPLAY
@item Return value: Any SCI type
@item Exception type: Symbol (identifier)
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
(switch state
  (case STATE_IDLE
    (print "Waiting")
  )
  (case STATE_ACTIVE
    (print "Running")
  )
)
@end example

@example
(procedure (find-item id)
  (if (= id -1)
    (throw 'not-found)
  )
  item
)
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Case Fall-Through}: Unlike C, SCI cases don't fall through; explicit jumps required.
@item @strong{Default Case}: Add @code{(case 0 @var{default-action})} or @code{(else @var{default})} for default.
@item @strong{Exception Handling}: @code{throw} without @code{catch} causes program termination.
@item @strong{Implicit Return}: If function doesn't explicitly return, last expression value is returned.
@item @strong{Multiple Returns}: Functions with multiple @code{return} statements are valid.
@end itemize

@node SCUMM Commands Reference
@section SCUMM Commands Reference

Script Creation Utility for Maniac Mansion (SCUMM) enables development of LucasArts-style point-and-click adventure games.
SCUMM provides 15+ essential commands for character animation, scene management, and game logic.

@menu
* SCUMM WALKTO::
* SCUMM PUTPIXEL::
* SCUMM PLAYSOUND::
* SCUMM LOADCOSTUME::
* SCUMM DRAW::
* SCUMM ERASE::
* SCUMM STOP::
* SCUMM STOPALL::
* SCUMM PAUSE::
* SCUMM RESUME::
* SCUMM VERB::
* SCUMM ACTOR::
* SCUMM OBJECT::
* SCUMM STATE::
* SCUMM VALUE::
@end menu

@node SCUMM WALKTO
@subsection SCUMM WALKTO

Move an actor to specified screen location.

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
(walkto @var{actor_id} @var{x} @var{y} @var{speed})
@end defsyn

@defsyn
(walkto @var{actor_id} @var{x} @var{y})
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

WALKTO moves an actor across the screen from current position to target coordinates.
The actor plays walk animation frames as it moves. Optional speed parameter controls movement rate.

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item Actor ID: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-15 typical, max 255)
@item X coordinate: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-319 for 320-pixel width)
@item Y coordinate: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-199 for 200-pixel height)
@item Speed: DECIMAL (1=slowest, 255=fastest; default 2)
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
@comment Move actor 1 to position (160, 100)
(walkto 1 160 100 2)
@end example

@example
@comment Slow walk to edge of screen
(walkto 1 319 100 1)
@end example

@example
@comment Fast exit
(walkto 1 0 100 255)
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Animation}: Actor plays walk cycle; ensure appropriate costume is loaded.
@item @strong{Blocking}: WALKTO is typically asynchronous; execution continues while actor walks.
@item @strong{Pathfinding}: Basic line-of-sight; no automatic obstacle avoidance.
@item @strong{Speed Variation}: Speed affects animation frame rate and pixel movement rate.
@item @strong{Coordinate System}: (0,0) is top-left; Y increases downward.
@end itemize

@node SCUMM PUTPIXEL
@subsection SCUMM PUTPIXEL

Draw a single pixel at specified coordinates.

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
(putpixel @var{x} @var{y} @var{color})
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

PUTPIXEL draws a single-pixel point on screen at coordinates (@var{x}, @var{y}) using specified color.
Useful for debugging and simple graphics.

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item X coordinate: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-319)
@item Y coordinate: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-199)
@item Color: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-15 in 16-color mode, 0-255 in 256-color)
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
@comment Draw red pixel at center
(putpixel 160 100 4)
@end example

@example
@comment Draw cross pattern
(putpixel 160 95 7)
(putpixel 160 105 7)
(putpixel 155 100 7)
(putpixel 165 100 7)
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Performance}: Drawing many pixels individually is slow; batch operations when possible.
@item @strong{Coordinates}: Must be within screen bounds (0-319, 0-199).
@item @strong{Color Palette}: Color values depend on current palette; same value may have different appearance.
@item @strong{Persistence}: Pixels drawn may be overwritten by background draws; draw after scene setup.
@item @strong{Debug Utility}: Primarily useful for debugging; avoid in production graphics.
@end itemize

@node SCUMM PLAYSOUND
@subsection SCUMM PLAYSOUND

Play a sound effect or music resource.

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
(playsound @var{sound_id})
@end defsyn

@defsyn
(playsound @var{sound_id} @var{priority})
@end defsyn

@defsyn
(playsound @var{sound_id} @var{priority} @var{volume})
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

PLAYSOUND triggers audio playback of a sound resource. Priority determines which sound wins if channels are full.
Volume controls output level.

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item Sound ID: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-255)
@item Priority: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-255; higher=more important)
@item Volume: BINARY UNSIGNED (0=silent, 255=maximum)
@item Channel: BINARY UNSIGNED (managed internally)
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
@comment Play footstep sound
(playsound 10)
@end example

@example
@comment Play high-priority music
(playsound 50 255)
@end example

@example
@comment Play quiet background sound
(playsound 30 128 64)
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Limited Channels}: Typically 4 audio channels; playback stops for lower-priority sounds.
@item @strong{Resource Loading}: Sound resources must be preloaded; missing resources produce silence.
@item @strong{Synchronization}: Sound playback is asynchronous; no automatic callback on completion.
@item @strong{Priority Starvation}: High-priority continuous sounds can prevent lower-priority sounds.
@item @strong{Volume Control}: Volume parameter overrides default resource volume.
@end itemize

@node SCUMM LOADCOSTUME
@subsection SCUMM LOADCOSTUME

Load character costume resource for animation.

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
(loadcostume @var{actor_id} @var{costume_id})
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

LOADCOSTUME loads a costume resource for an actor. Costumes define the visual appearance,
animation frames, and behavior for characters. Must be called before using walk/talk animations.

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item Actor ID: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-15 typical)
@item Costume ID: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-255)
@item Resource type: Internally SCUMM costume resource
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
@comment Load player costume
(loadcostume 1 50)
@end example

@example
@comment Load NPC costume
(loadcostume 5 75)
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Resource Loading}: Costume must be in game resource archive; loading non-existent ID fails silently.
@item @strong{Persistence}: Once loaded, costume remains until explicitly changed.
@item @strong{Animation Frames}: Costume defines available animation frames (walk, talk, object interactions).
@item @strong{Palette}: Costume may include palette information; loading new costume may change colors.
@item @strong{Memory}: Keeping many costumes loaded consumes memory; unload unused costumes.
@end itemize

@node SCUMM DRAW
@subsection SCUMM DRAW

Render object or actor on screen.

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
(draw @var{actor_id})
@end defsyn

@defsyn
(draw @var{object_id} @var{x} @var{y})
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

DRAW renders an actor or object on screen at its current or specified position.
Called automatically for most actors, but may be needed for manual control.

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item Actor/Object ID: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-255)
@item X coordinate: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-319)
@item Y coordinate: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-199)
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
@comment Draw actor 1 at current position
(draw 1)
@end example

@example
@comment Draw object at specific location
(draw 50 160 100)
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Automatic Drawing}: Most SCUMM actors draw automatically; manual DRAW rarely needed.
@item @strong{Z-Order}: Drawing order affects layering; actors drawn later appear on top.
@item @strong{Performance}: Excessive DRAW calls reduce performance; use only when necessary.
@item @strong{Invalidation}: Screen areas may be invalidated after DRAW; complete scene redraws next cycle.
@item @strong{Costume Requirements}: Actors must have valid costume loaded before drawing.
@end itemize

@node SCUMM ERASE
@subsection SCUMM ERASE

Remove actor or object from screen (hide without deletion).

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
(erase @var{actor_id})
@end defsyn

@defsyn
(erase @var{object_id})
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

ERASE hides an actor or object from the screen without removing it from memory.
The actor/object remains in game state but is not visually rendered.

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item Actor/Object ID: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-255)
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
@comment Hide actor 1
(erase 1)
@end example

@example
@comment Hide object 50
(erase 50)
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Persistence}: ERASE hides but doesn't delete; actor/object state is preserved.
@item @strong{Redraw}: Next DRAW call will show the actor/object again.
@item @strong{Screen Invalidation}: Erased areas are redrawn with background; may cause flicker.
@item @strong{Cleanup}: Use ERASE for temporary visibility changes; DELETE for permanent removal.
@item @strong{Performance}: ERASing many objects reduces screen update time.
@end itemize

@node SCUMM STOP
@subsection SCUMM STOP

Stop animation or script execution for specified actor.

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
(stop @var{actor_id})
@end defsyn

@defsyn
(stop @var{actor_id} @var{animation_type})
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

STOP halts animation and movement for an actor. Without parameters, it stops all activity.
With animation type, it stops specific animation class (walk, talk, object use).

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item Actor ID: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-15 typical)
@item Animation type: BINARY UNSIGNED (0=all, 1=walk, 2=talk, 3=other)
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
@comment Stop actor 1 completely
(stop 1)
@end example

@example
@comment Stop walk animation only
(stop 1 1)
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Incomplete Stop}: Stopping one animation type may leave others running.
@item @strong{Resume}: RESUME or new animations restart activity.
@item @strong{State Preservation}: Actor state is preserved; STOP doesn't change variables.
@item @strong{Visual Frame}: Actor remains visible at current frame; uses last animation state.
@item @strong{Timing}: STOP takes effect at next animation frame boundary, not immediately.
@end itemize

@node SCUMM STOPALL
@subsection SCUMM STOPALL

Stop all animations and movements (global command).

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
(stopall)
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

STOPALL halts all active animations, movements, and sounds globally. Used for cutscenes,
transitions, and critical game pauses.

@subsubsection Examples

@example
@comment Freeze all actors for cutscene
(stopall)
@end example

@example
@comment Prepare for scene transition
(stopall)
(loadscene 5)
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Global Effect}: Stops ALL actors; may be too aggressive for selective pause.
@item @strong{State Preservation}: All state variables are preserved; STOPALL is reversible.
@item @strong{Performance}: STOPALL may cause frame rate spike by invalidating many screen regions.
@item @strong{Partial Resume}: No built-in "resume all"; use individual WALK/ANIM commands.
@item @strong{Synchronization}: Useful for synchronizing cutscene elements.
@end itemize

@node SCUMM PAUSE
@subsection SCUMM PAUSE

Pause game execution (input disabled, animation frozen).

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
(pause)
@end defsyn

@defsyn
(pause @var{duration_frames})
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

PAUSE suspends all game activity for a specified duration or indefinitely.
Player input is ignored; animations and scripts freeze. Useful for cut-scenes and delays.

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item Duration: BINARY UNSIGNED (frames; 0=indefinite)
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
@comment Pause indefinitely (until RESUME called)
(pause)
@end example

@example
@comment Pause for 60 frames (~1 second at 60 FPS)
(pause 60)
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Input Blocking}: Player cannot interact while paused; ensure timeout or explicit RESUME.
@item @strong{Animation Freezing}: All animations freeze at current frame; time stops.
@item @strong{Sound}: Audio typically continues during pause (pause doesn't mute).
@item @strong{Indefinite Pause}: PAUSE without duration requires explicit RESUME.
@item @strong{Frame Counting}: Duration is measured in engine frames, not seconds.
@end itemize

@node SCUMM RESUME
@subsection SCUMM RESUME

Resume game execution after PAUSE.

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
(resume)
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

RESUME unfreezes all game activity after a PAUSE command. Animations resume from current frame,
scripts continue, and player input becomes active again.

@subsubsection Examples

@example
@comment Resume after pause
(pause 120)
@comment ... wait ...
(resume)
@end example

@example
@comment Pause then resume in response to player action
(pause)
@comment ... wait for key press ...
(resume)
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Idempotent}: RESUME on already-running game is safe (no-op).
@item @strong{Animation State}: Resumes with stored animation state; no frame advancement during pause.
@item @strong{Time Accounting}: Paused time is not counted against frame budgets; time synchronization needed.
@item @strong{Input Queue}: Input events during pause are typically discarded; no buffering.
@item @strong{Script Position}: Scripts resume from point before PAUSE; execution flow is continuous.
@end itemize

@node SCUMM VERB
@subsection SCUMM VERB

Define or trigger interactive verb (action).

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
(verb @var{verb_id} "@var{verb_name}" @var{key_code})
@end defsyn

@defsyn
(verb @var{verb_id})
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

VERBs define interactive actions available to the player (Look, Use, Take, Talk). Each verb
is assigned a unique ID, display name, and keyboard shortcut. The verb system enables
point-and-click adventure game interactions.

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item Verb ID: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-15 typical)
@item Verb name: DISPLAY (ASCII string, 12 chars max)
@item Key code: BINARY UNSIGNED (ASCII code or special key)
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
@comment Define verb 1 as LOOK with L key
(verb 1 "Look" x'4C')
@end example

@example
@comment Define verb 2 as USE with U key
(verb 2 "Use" x'55')
@end example

@example
@comment Trigger verb 1
(verb 1)
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Verb Pool}: Limited number of verbs (typically 0-15); allocate carefully.
@item @strong{Visual Display}: Verbs are displayed in verb toolbar; position may be layout-dependent.
@item @strong{Verb Redefinition}: Redefining a verb ID replaces old definition.
@item @strong{Input Handling}: Verb key presses must be routed through input system.
@item @strong{Verb Scripting}: Verb selection triggers associated script; implement verb handlers.
@end itemize

@node SCUMM ACTOR
@subsection SCUMM ACTOR

Configure actor properties and behavior.

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
(actor @var{actor_id} AT @var{x} @var{y})
@end defsyn

@defsyn
(actor @var{actor_id} COSTUME @var{costume_id})
@end defsyn

@defsyn
(actor @var{actor_id} NAME "@var{name}")
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

ACTOR commands configure actor state: position, costume, name, palette, and other properties.
Used for initialization and runtime behavior modification.

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item Actor ID: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-15 typical)
@item X coordinate: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-319)
@item Y coordinate: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-199)
@item Costume ID: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-255)
@item Name: DISPLAY (ASCII string)
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
@comment Position actor 1 at (160, 100)
(actor 1 AT 160 100)
@end example

@example
@comment Change costume
(actor 1 COSTUME 75)
@end example

@example
@comment Set actor name
(actor 1 NAME "John")
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Property Modification}: Actor commands don't require full reconfiguration; modify individual properties.
@item @strong{Persistence}: Properties persist until explicitly changed; initialization required at game start.
@item @strong{Costume Requirements}: Actor must have valid costume loaded before position changes are visible.
@item @strong{Name Display}: Actor names used for dialogue attribution and UI.
@item @strong{Palette}: Each actor may have associated palette; costume loads may change colors.
@end itemize

@node SCUMM OBJECT
@subsection SCUMM OBJECT

Manage game object properties and behavior.

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
(object @var{object_id} AT @var{x} @var{y})
@end defsyn

@defsyn
(object @var{object_id} IMAGE @var{image_id})
@end defsyn

@defsyn
(object @var{object_id} NAME "@var{name}")
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

OBJECT commands configure static and dynamic objects in the game world. Objects are non-actor
entities: doors, chests, furniture, etc. Each object has position, image, name, and state.

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item Object ID: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-255)
@item X coordinate: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-319)
@item Y coordinate: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-199)
@item Image ID: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-255)
@item Name: DISPLAY (ASCII string)
@item State: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-255)
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
@comment Place door object at location
(object 10 AT 300 150)
@end example

@example
@comment Set object image
(object 10 IMAGE 100)
@end example

@example
@comment Name object for player reference
(object 10 NAME "Door")
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Object Limits}: Typically 0-255 objects; fewer in earlier games (older systems).
@item @strong{Persistence}: Objects remain until explicitly deleted; state persists through scenes.
@item @strong{Interaction}: Objects must be defined before players can interact with them.
@item @strong{Image Resources}: Object images must be preloaded; missing images display as blank.
@item @strong{State Tracking}: Use object STATE values to track object interactions (locked/unlocked, opened/closed).
@end itemize

@node SCUMM STATE
@subsection SCUMM STATE

Query or set object/actor state value.

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
(state @var{object_id} @var{state_value})
@end defsyn

@defsyn
(state @var{object_id})
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

STATE retrieves or sets a numeric state value for an object or actor.
State values track logical properties: door locked/open, light on/off, etc.

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item Object/Actor ID: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-255)
@item State value: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-255; meaning game-dependent)
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
@comment Set door state to locked (1)
(state 10 1)
@end example

@example
@comment Query door state
(if (state 10)
  (print "Door is locked")
)
@end example

@example
@comment Toggle object state
(if (state 5)
  (state 5 0)
  (state 5 1)
)
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{State Semantics}: State meaning is game-defined; document state value interpretation.
@item @strong{Persistence}: State persists with object; saves/loads preserve state.
@item @strong{Image Mapping}: Some engines map state to different images (open vs. closed door).
@item @strong{Interaction Gating}: Use state to control interaction availability.
@item @strong{Query Efficiency}: Querying state is O(1); no performance penalty.
@end itemize

@node SCUMM VALUE
@subsection SCUMM VALUE

Store and retrieve numeric values associated with objects/actors.

@subsubsection Syntax

@defsyn
(value @var{object_id} @var{numeric_value})
@end defsyn

@defsyn
(value @var{object_id})
@end defsyn

@subsubsection Description

VALUE stores arbitrary numeric data with objects/actors. Unlike STATE (which has game-semantic
interpretation), VALUE is pure data storage for custom numeric attributes (health, charge, counter).

@subsubsection Numeric Types

@itemize
@item Object/Actor ID: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-255)
@item Numeric value: BINARY UNSIGNED (0-65535) or BINARY SIGNED (-32768 to 32767)
@end itemize

@subsubsection Examples

@example
@comment Store health value
(value 1 100)
@end example

@example
@comment Retrieve and test health
(if (< (value 1) 50)
  (print "Low health")
)
@end example

@example
@comment Damage actor
(value 1 (- (value 1) 10))
@end example

@subsubsection Gotchas and Features

@itemize
@item @strong{Semantics}: VALUE is semantically neutral; no built-in interpretation.
@item @strong{Persistence}: VALUES persist with objects across saves/loads.
@item @strong{Type Flexibility}: VALUES can store any numeric value; ensure consistent interpretation.
@item @strong{Range}: Different implementations may support 8-bit, 16-bit, or 32-bit values.
@item @strong{Array Equivalent}: Multiple VALUEs can form array-like structures.
@end itemize
